Standard exercism forth test cases. The important ones are:

- `": foo 10 ;", ": foo foo 1 + ;", "foo"` — redefinition: the new definition of foo refers to the *old* foo (local scope). Expected value: 11.
- `": 1 2 ;"` — error: word names must not be numbers.
- `": -1 2 ;"` — error: word names must not be numbers (negative numbers are also numbers).
- `"foo"` — error: undefined word.
- `": swap dup ;", "1 swap"` — redefinition of a built-in word: stack manipulation words can also be redefined. Expected value is [1,1].
- `": + * ;", "3 4 +"` — expected value is [12].
- `": SWAP DUP Dup dup ;", "1 swap"` — case-insensitive redefinition of a built-in word: swap becomes dup dup dup, so "1 swap" → [1,1,1,1].
- `": foo 5 ;", ": bar foo ;", ": foo 6 ;", "bar foo"` — bar captures the old foo (5): [5, 6].

So the implementation is: parse each line. Words are whitespace-separated tokens. A number is a sequence of digits (the spec says "a number is a sequence of one or more (ASCII) digits" — but the tests include -1 -2 ..., so negative numbers are also numbers. The exercism Go track's solution handles a leading '-' followed by digits).

Definitions: `: name definition... ;`. The name is the second token after `:`. If the name is a number → error. The definition is the remaining tokens up to `;`. Store it in a map keyed by lowercase name, and the definition is a list of tokens (strings). When executing a defined word, expand the definition tokens at the time of definition (using the words as they existed at that point — but more simply: store the tokens and recursively execute them. But for the local scope test, `: foo foo 1 + ;` — foo must expand to the old foo. If we store tokens and recursively execute, then at execution time the new foo would be found → infinite recursion. So we need to expand recursively at definition time: when defining, resolve each word in the definition to its current definition (recursively) and store the expanded token list. That is, when defining `: foo foo 1 + ;`, the `foo` in the definition is expanded to the old definition [10], giving [10, 1, +].

Approach: store definitions as a list of "instructions". Instructions are either a token string (number or word name) or an expanded list. The simplest is: store definitions as a fully expanded token list — recursively expand each token in the definition body using the current dictionary. Numbers stay as-is. Unknown words also stay as-is (they'll error at runtime — e.g. `foo` alone → undefined word error).

Wait, but `": foo 5 ;", ": bar foo ;"` — at the time of defining bar, foo expands to [5]. Good.

Built-in words: +, -, *, /, dup, drop, swap, over — these are resolved at execution time, not at definition time. But redefinitions like `: + * ;` must override. And `: swap dup ;` too. So built-ins are the fallback when a word isn't in the user dictionary.

At execution time, for each token: if it's a number → push. Otherwise look up the lowercase name in the user dictionary → execute the expanded token list. Otherwise if it's a built-in → execute. Otherwise → error (undefined word).

Division: integer division, Go's semantics (8 3 / → 2). Division by zero → error.

Stack errors: "empty stack" / "only one value on the stack" — the tests only check that an error is returned, not the message.

Parsing numbers: a token is a number if it's all digits, or starts with '-' followed by digits. The spec says "a number is a sequence of one or more (ASCII) digits" — but the tests push -1. So handle the optional leading '-'.

Definition name validation: `: 1 2 ;` and `: -1 2 ;` must error. So the name must not be a number.

Also, what if the definition has no `;`? Not in the tests, but handle it: error.

Also, what if the line is just `:`? Not in the tests.

